steps.message:
Done. Implemented with TDD — every behavior had a test written first and watched fail before any production code.

**`src/utils.js`** — the new function alongside `greet`:

```js
function isValidEmail(value) {
  if (typeof value !== 'string') {
    return false;
  }
  const atIndex = value.indexOf('@');
  if (atIndex <= 0) {
    return false;
  }
  return value.slice(atIndex + 1).includes('.');
}
```

`atIndex <= 0` covers both "no `@`" (-1) and "nothing before the `@`" (0) in one check. The dot is searched only in the slice after the `@`, so `first.last@example` is correctly rejected.

**`test/utils.test.js`** — 7 tests, all passing. **`package.json`** — added `"test": "node --test test/"`.

The red-green cycles, each verified failing before implementing:

| Cycle | Test | Failure observed |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | accepts `user@example.com` | `isValidEmail is not a function` |
| 2 | rejects `user.example.com` | got `true`, expected `false` |
| 3 | rejects `@example.com` | got `true`, expected `false` |
| 4 | rejects `user@example`, `first.last@example` | got `true`, expected `false` |
| 5 | rejects `undefined`/`null`/`42` | `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` |

Two notes on what I did beyond the literal spec:

- **Test framework:** the repo had none and no `node_modules`, so I used Node's built-in `node:test` to keep the project dependency-free, matching its zero-dependency `package.json`.
- **Non-string input:** your spec says "return true/false", but the function threw on `null`/`undefined`, so I added a type guard. Tell me if you'd rather it throw on bad input.

One thing this deliberately does *not* do: it isn't RFC-compliant email validation. `a@b.c`, `user@.com`, and `user@example.` all pass, since they satisfy your three rules. That's what you asked for, and it's the right call for most signup-form use cases — but say the word if you want the domain-part rules tightened.
